### Manual requests for the ComputeSeparation Aspire harness.
### The fake platform sends the platform requests: it assigns the pod on the WorkerProxy, assigns the placeholder Host
### to the app, and links the worker to the Host.
### Its /simulate routes are harness-only; the WorkerProxy and Host routes they call are the real contracts.
###
### Run AppHost with the `project-placeholder-manual` launch profile (F5, or the command below). It pins the ports used
### here and turns off automatic assign and link so you can send each step yourself, in order.
###
###   dotnet run --project tools\ComputeSeparation\AppHost\ComputeSeparation.AppHost.csproj --launch-profile project-placeholder-manual
###
### To add a second worker after the Host is serving, use `project-placeholder-manual-multiple-workers`.
### Complete steps 1 to 4 first, then steps 5 to 7. Both worker/proxy pairs start immediately, but links remain manual.
###
###   dotnet run --project tools\ComputeSeparation\AppHost\ComputeSeparation.AppHost.csproj --launch-profile project-placeholder-manual-multiple-workers

@platform = http://localhost:7060
@host = http://127.0.0.1:7071
@workerProxy = http://localhost:7081
@secondWorkerProxy = http://localhost:7091

### Expected flow, in order.

### 1. The platform assigns the pod on the WorkerProxy. Expect 201, or 204 if repeated.
### 503 WorkerNotReady means the sample worker has not connected yet; retry.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/worker-proxy-1/assign

### Optional: the WorkerProxy pod state is now ReadyForRequest.
GET {{workerProxy}}/admin/worker/state

### 2. The platform assigns the placeholder Host to the app (POST /admin/instance/assign). Expect 202, or 409 if
### repeated. Specialization applies configuration without waiting for the worker link.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/host/assign

### Optional: inspect the first published Host state before linking.
### Expect capacity 0 and linkedWorkerCount 0 after specialization; 204 means no publication has arrived yet.
GET {{platform}}/simulate/host/state

### 3. The platform links the worker to the Host. Expect 201, or 200 for a matching retry.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/worker-proxy-1/link

### Optional: inspect the latest published capacity.
### Expect { "createdTime": "<UTC timestamp>", "httpCapacity": 16, "linkedWorkerCount": 1 } after the worker is ready.
### This sample explicitly assigns maxHttpConcurrency:16; product capacity is the sum of ready HTTP workers' assigned limits.
### Linked counts can arrive before readiness. Retry while capacity is 0.
### Distinct snapshots can have equal or earlier timestamps. The fake records arrival order, not timestamp order.
GET {{platform}}/simulate/host/state

### 4. A client invokes the function through Host -> WorkerProxy -> worker.
GET {{host}}/api/hello

### The remaining requests require `project-placeholder-manual-multiple-workers`.
### Wait for step 4 to succeed before linking the second worker, so this exercises a genuinely late link.

### 5. Assign the second pod. Expect 201, or 204 if repeated; retry 503 until its worker has connected.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/worker-proxy-2/assign

### Optional: inspect the second pod independently.
GET {{secondWorkerProxy}}/admin/worker/state

### 6. Link the second worker to the existing Host. Expect 201, or 200 for a matching retry.
### Do not send /simulate/host/assign again: the same Host and indexed functions are reused.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/worker-proxy-2/link

### Optional: inspect the capacity contributed by both assigned workers.
### Expect httpCapacity 32 and linkedWorkerCount 2 once both workers are ready.
GET {{platform}}/simulate/host/state

### Optional: duplicate the second link and inspect again.
### Expect 200; the matching link does not add capacity or create a new snapshot.
POST {{platform}}/simulate/worker/worker-proxy-2/link

###
GET {{platform}}/simulate/host/state

### 7. Repeat this request to route invocations across both worker/proxy pairs.
### Compare "Worker ID:" in the response body; each worker has an independently generated ID.
### Watch HTTP forwarding in the `worker-proxy-1` and `worker-proxy-2` console logs in the dashboard.
GET {{host}}/api/hello
